<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gia Long</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Nguyen Anh, Nguyễn Phúc Ánh, Nguyễn Ánh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gia Long, born Nguyen Phuc Anh, was a survivor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> who fought the Tay Son for over two decades from bases in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta, with help from Siam and the French missionary Pigneau de Behaine. After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thang Long</a> in 1802 he proclaimed himself emperor, founding the Nguyen dynasty and ruling the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from Hue, where he built a centralised Confucian state and a great citadel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gia-long.jpg" alt="Gia Long" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, restored by Nguyễn Phước Vĩnh Khánh ([1]) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Gia_Long.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
